{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space E with its Borel measurable structure (including dimension zero), every measurable space S, every real kappa>=1, and every twice continuously Frechet differentiable V:E->R such that, for all x,v in E, kappa^(-1)*||v||^2 <= D^2 V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2, consider any measurable eta:S->R and y:S->E satisfying eta(s)>0 for every s. There exists a single measurable function p:S->E such that p(s)+eta(s)*grad V(p(s))=y(s) for all s. Using that same p everywhere, define\nrho_s(u)=V(p(s)+sqrt(eta(s))*u)-V(p(s))-sqrt(eta(s))*<grad V(p(s)),u>,\nmu=volume.tilted(x |-> -V(x)),\nR_s=mu.tilted(x |-> -||x-y(s)||^2/(2*eta(s))),\nr_s=(R_s).map(x |-> sqrt(eta(s))^(-1)*(x-p(s))),\ngamma=stdGaussian E,\nZ_s=integral_E exp(-rho_s(u)) d gamma(u),\nq_s(u)=exp(-rho_s(u))/Z_s,\nf_s(u)=exp(-rho_s(u)/2)/sqrt(Z_s).\nHere volume is canonical Haar/Lebesgue measure, gamma is the centered covariance-identity Gaussian, tilted is the genuine normalized exponential tilt (zero when nonintegrable), map is the actual measurable pushforward, and withDensity uses a nonnegative extended-real density. Then for every s in S, simultaneously:\n(1) 0 < Z_s <= 1.\n(2) The actual pushforward r_s equals gamma.tilted(u |-> -rho_s(u)) and equals gamma.withDensity(u |-> ofReal(q_s(u))).\n(3) r_s is a probability measure.\n(4) For every u in E, q_s(u)>0 and f_s(u)^2=q_s(u), and integral_E q_s d gamma=1.\n(5) f_s is twice continuously differentiable in u; f_s and grad f_s belong to L^2(gamma); grad rho_s belongs to L^2(r_s).\n(6) u |-> q_s(u)*log(q_s(u)) is genuinely integrable against gamma.\n(7) For every u, grad f_s(u)=-(f_s(u)/2)*grad rho_s(u), and grad(log q_s)(u)=-grad rho_s(u).\n(8) integral_E ||grad f_s(u)||^2 d gamma(u) = (1/4)*integral_E ||grad rho_s(u)||^2 d r_s(u).\nGradients and Hessians are in the real Hilbert space E, and all vector multiplications displayed above are real scalar multiplications. The same existential measurable p determines the stationary equation, residual potential, and recentering map. These conclusions hold for arbitrary positive eta(s), with no upper cap and no uniform bound or integrability requirement on the parameter family. No stationary point or output law, normalizer, probability, entropy integral, gradient identity, or moment bound is provided as an extra premise. The assertion supplies measurability of p and pointwise C^2 regularity of f_s; it does not explicitly assert joint smoothness in the abstract parameter s or uniqueness of p. A constant family indexed by a singleton gives every fixed positive parameter. The energy coefficient is exactly 1/4 with unscaled squared-gradient integrals.",
